Pinia is a state management library
or in other words a way to store and send information to and from components in vue

Pinia has and is a store, that you can use for one or more pieces of information

every piece of info in the store is a "Source of truth"

Components can look at the info in the Pinia store and change it or verify it

It works with typescript under normal circumstances

You can do things at the same time with the Pinia Store

a pinia store is usually stored in the "Stores" folder in a vue project

The state is the information in the store, you can implement them right then or you can add references
to other places in order to locate the store

in the components under the computed section of the export you can look, retrieve, or change
items in the pinia store

in the pinia store you can return items

actions are methods that you can use within the store that can be called in other components.
(Think your api calls in WPL project)

actions are usually if not always async

mutations have influence on actions

modules have a weird structure in a project

to make a store:
 -you just make a new javascript file in your stores folder

 -import from define store in order to declare it a store

 -export const: this is how you use your store elsewhere, you give a name and an ID to your store,
 and options for your store when you export it

 -from here you can declare a state, which can have items or not, but in most cases there is a
 "default", you can store arrays or objects or anything really, so you can have a state item that's an array
 then directly after that a string like a title or something

export const useCardStore = defineStore('cardStore', {
    state: () => ({
        cards: [],
        card1: {
            name: '',
            manaCost: 0,
            imgSource: '',
            description: '',
        },
        card2: {
            name: '',
            manaCost: 0,
            imgSource: '',
            description: '',
        },
        loading: true,
    }),
    actions: {}
}

-you can use getters to return your state items, or the length of any arrays and such
GETTERS ARE SYNCHRONOUS

-then you can use Actions to change your state items. (API calls remember)
ACTIONS MAY BE ASYNCHRONOUS, API CALLS GO HERE


Now you can use your store in other components by declaring it as a variable and then either calling
the actions or calling the getters or state. you can declare it in your data of your component

You can call the actions within the methods of the components.

